usage:
import { ActionNode } from "./ActionNode";
import "./ActionNode.css";

<ActionNode
  bounce={20}
  stagger={55}
  count={4}
  corner={20}
/>

notes:
/* Every button is struck from ONE point: the card's top-right
   corner. The fan is an arc drawn from it, so the angle is the
   only thing that separates one button from the next and the
   distance is the only thing that animates. */
.nod-fan { position: absolute; width: 0; height: 0; }

/* the anchor is a POINT, so each button's own centre goes on
   it first and the travel is measured from there */
.nod-act {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0; left: 0;
  margin-top: -14px;
  margin-left: -14px;
}

/* and in the component, per button, driven by one spring each.
   The circle is centred 26px INSIDE the corner rather than on
   it, which is what lets the radius be big enough to space
   four buttons while still passing close to both edges — so
   they ride round the corner's curve rather than standing off
   its point, and the angular step can be a constant:

     const rho = 26 + gap + 14;          // inset, air, radius
     const deg = 45 + 43 * (n - 1) / 2 - 43 * i;
     const d   = rho * (spring / 100);

     translate: `${Math.cos(deg * Math.PI / 180) * d}px
                 ${-Math.sin(deg * Math.PI / 180) * d}px`
     scale:     0.35 + 0.65 * Math.min(u, 1)

   Nothing in CSS transitions any of those three. The spring
   owns position, size and opacity together; a transition on
   top of it would be two animations arguing over one
   property. */
